= Introduction

Artificial intelligence (AI) image synthesis is the process of generating new images by learning patterns from existing data. It is used in digital content creation, image editing, and visual design. However, generating realistic, diverse, and high-resolution images remains challenging. Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) can generate images quickly, but their training may be unstable or produce limited variation. Autoregressive models provide an explicit probabilistic description of images, although sequential generation can be slow @goodfellow2014gan @salimans2016gan @oord2016pixelrnn.

Diffusion models use a different approach. A fixed forward process gradually adds noise to training images, while a neural network learns information needed to reverse this process. Once trained, the model can generate new images starting from random noise. Ho et al. @ho2020ddpm show that predicting added noise with a simplified objective can produce high-quality samples. Their pixel-space Denoising Diffusion Probabilistic Model (DDPM) requires many denoising steps, making generation expensive. Rombach et al. @rombach2022ldm reduce the cost of each step by applying diffusion to compressed image representations in Latent Diffusion Models (LDM).

This report addresses the question: how can learning to remove noise enable a model to generate new images? We explain the forward and reverse processes, calculate a one-dimensional example, and examine five noise levels on a $28 times 28$ image. We then describe latent compression and cross-attention conditioning, and compare small pixel-space and latent-space implementations using saved MNIST experiment records. The comparison considers representation size, estimated arithmetic, recorded runtime, reconstruction, and conditioning. These experiments illustrate the mechanisms rather than reproduce the original papers' image-quality results. We also discuss limitations related to compression, data, copyright, and bias.
